  "briefing": {
    "generated_at_utc": "2026-09-05T09:24:11Z",
    "analysts": {
      "body": "This week, Wall Street talk kept coming back to Tesla’s new Cybercab and to the race to build AI data centers. Dan Ives said on CNBC that the clash over U.S. AI data centers — tech companies vs. Washington — will stay a big theme into year-end as those huge sites keep getting built.\n\nGary Black thought Tesla’s Austin Cybercab event fell short of the hype. Tesla mostly said the car will join the robotaxi fleet, then let guests ride in the two-seat cabin with no pedals. He also noted how unusual it is for a company worth about $1.3 trillion to stay this expensive for so long, while bulls and bears still argue about when cars can drive with no human backup.\n\nCathie Wood stayed upbeat on self-driving and AI. She highlighted the idea that robotaxis could take trips away from Uber-style apps, and said many investors still don’t grasp how fast AI usage has grown — even while AI revenue is climbing.",
      "sources": [
        {
          "name": "Ives",
          "handle": "DivesTech",
          "url": "https://x.com/DivesTech/status/2095610104133321005",
          "date": "2026-09-03"
        },
        {
